// PENDING: from an OPEN SigmaHQ pull request. Not reviewed or merged by SigmaHQ yet.
// Pull request: #6293 new: Redis Server Process Writing To SSH Authorized Keys File
// Pull request URL: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/pull/6293
// Pull request author: PixelatedContinuum
// Change: new rule
// Title: Redis Server Process Writing To SSH Authorized Keys File
// Sigma ID: 5a6dff70-3536-49cb-b14a-f39da78d417c
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: The Hunters Ledger
// Date: 2026-07-17 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: persistence, privilege-escalation, T1098.004
// Description: Detects the redis-server process writing to a user's SSH authorized_keys file. An
//   unauthenticated Redis instance can be made to write its database to an arbitrary path via CONFIG
//   SET dir and CONFIG SET dbfilename followed by SAVE, which is used to append an attacker-
//   controlled SSH public key and gain persistent shell access. A legitimate Redis deployment does
//   not write to this path.
// False positives:
//   - Unlikely. No legitimate Redis deployment configuration writes to this file
// References:
//   - https://hacktricks.wiki/en/network-services-pentesting/6379-pentesting-redis.html
//   - https://the-hunters-ledger.com/hunting-detections/seasia-gov-exploitation-toolkit-144-172-106-236-detections/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/a3b8eddb0eb40b37b6a359cd34d4cdd994325e81/rules/linux/file_event/file_event_lnx_redis_authorized_keys_write.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceFileEvents
| where InitiatingProcessFolderPath endswith "/redis-server" and FolderPath endswith "/.ssh/authorized_keys"
